      "math_academy_id": "q-217254",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3.5$ |\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| $14$ | $1$ |\n\nGiven the table above, select the options that make the following statement true:\n\nThe variable $y$ is {{field-1}} proportional to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=$  {{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3.5$ | $4\\cdot 3.5=14$ |\n| $7$ | $2$ | $7\\cdot 2=14$ |\n| $14$ | $1$ | $14\\cdot 1=14$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=14.$ Therefore, we conclude:\n\nThe variable $y$ is $\\text{inversely}$ proportional  to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=14.$",
